module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.IntegralClosure.IntegrallyClosed
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.AtPrime.Basic

@[expose] public section

universe u v

/-- Normality of a supremum from a family covering the base by normal fibers.
Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0333, final localization argument for the subring
generated by the finite collection. (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-finite-normal-cover-sup-normal)

Proof: Put S = sup s. Since integralClosure A K is a subalgebra and contains each
member, it contains S. Thus every element of S is integral over A. Given a prime q
of S, contract it to p in A and use hcover to choose C in s normal at every prime
over p. The inclusion C -> S contracts q to a prime r of C lying over p, so C_r is
normal. Both C and S have fraction field K, since they lie between A and K.
Every element of S satisfies a monic polynomial over A, hence over C and C_r.
Localize S at the image of C \ r. This ring embeds in K and is integral over C_r:
its numerators are integral and its denominators are units of C_r. Normality of C_r
therefore makes this localized ring equal to C_r inside K. The ring S_q is a further
localization, since C \ r maps into S \ q. All its denominators are nonzero in K.
A localization of an integrally closed domain at nonzero denominators is integrally
closed, giving the claimed normality of S_q. -/
theorem finite_normal_cover_sup_normal (A : Type u) (K : Type v)
    [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [Field K] [Algebra A K] [IsFractionRing A K]
    (s : Finset (Subalgebra A K))
    (hs : ∀ C ∈ s, C ≤ integralClosure A K)
    (hcover : ∀ (p : Ideal A) [p.IsPrime], ∃ C ∈ s,
      ∀ (q : Ideal C) [q.IsPrime], Ideal.comap (algebraMap A C) q = p →
        IsIntegrallyClosed (Localization.AtPrime q)) :
    ∀ (q : Ideal (s.sup (fun C : Subalgebra A K => C) : Subalgebra A K)) [q.IsPrime],
      IsIntegrallyClosed (Localization.AtPrime q) := by
  sorry
